From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:01:17 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] 3D viewer: a translucent model counts in the depth fit by its
 share of each pixel

The separation is the largest under which every pixel of the view stays
inside the person's depth limits, a translucent pixel's depth being the mean
of what it shows, each weighted by its share of the pixel's colour.  The
transparent 3D models are drawn without writing depth, so the first pass
from the camera between the eyes did not see a translucent model standing
in front of everything else, and its pixels could pass the popped limit.

That pass is now drawn into an off-screen float framebuffer.  Before the
transparent models it reads the depth of what is drawn and writes each
pixel's distance into the colour, the background at the screen.  The
transparent models are then drawn as the eyes draw them, in the same order,
with the same alpha and blending, but each fragment's colour is its own
distance: the texture stage gives black and keeps the model's alpha, and
linear fog over the distance adds it.  Blended so, every pixel ends as the
contribution-weighted mean distance of what it shows; what is drawn after
them (the grid) keeps its own depth.  Without a float framebuffer the
transparent models write depth in that pass and count at their own
distance, which keeps every pixel inside the limits.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 via Claude Code
